TI  - Semantic Entropy and Uncertainty in Large Language Models for Nursing: A Scoping Review
AB  - This scoping review aims to map and synthesize the available scientific evidence on semantic entropy and linguistic uncertainty in Large Language Models (LLMs) applied to nursing practice and education. As generative AI tools are increasingly integrated into clinical decision support and patient communication, understanding their "epistemic uncertainty" is crucial for patient safety. Objectives: To identify how current literature characterizes semantic entropy, model hallucinations, and linguistic consistency in nursing-related outputs; to explore the implications of these uncertainties for nursing workflows and clinical decision-making. Methodology: Following JBI guidelines and the PRISMA-ScR checklist, this study will search PubMed/MEDLINE, BVS Nursing (LILACS), SCIELO, LILACS and DOAJ for studies published between 2023 and 2026. Expected Results: The review expects to identify a critical gap in standardized protocols for evaluating AI reliability in nursing. The findings will provide a theoretical framework for nurses to critically assess AI-generated information, reducing risks of misinformation in clinical settings
