TI  - Framing dependence and confidence calibration in large language model evaluation of statistical evidence
AB  - Three hypotheses concerning large language model (LLM) reasoning were registered on 1 September 2026 and tested against independently instantiated agents of a single model family. The first — that framing determines interpretation — was supported: byte-identical statistical results received differing verdicts when the surrounding claim was described as "well-established" rather than "fringe." The second — that stated confidence is insensitive to whether the queried object exists — was falsified: confidence fell by 92.4 points (95.6 to 3.2; Welch t = 236.7, p = 3.4e-30) on fabricated citations, all 15 of which were correctly declined. The third — that self-grading is inflated — was not measurable, as subject performance saturated the response scale. Version 1 reported the first result as degenerate between framing bias and appropriate prior-sensitivity and specified a discriminating experiment. Version 2 reports that experiment. When subjects were instructed explicitly to judge the evidence alone, independent of the claim's plausibility, verdicts still moved on 5 of 9 paired comparisons (p = 3.3e-5) — while, in the same responses, stated belief moved 73.2 points in the direction the framing warranted. The prior-sensitivity reading is therefore rejected: subjects demonstrably can separate likelihood from posterior, apply the separation correctly to belief, and still admit the prior into the evidence judgment after being told to exclude it. The effect is not removable by clarification. A replication arm returned 3 of 9 flips against version 1's 6 of 9, indicating that the direction of the effect is robust while its magnitude at this sample size is not.
