TI  - Large language models for climate vulnerability assessment: a reproducible framework with uncertainty quantification
AB  - Climate vulnerability assessment faces significant challenges in integrating heterogeneous data sources while maintaining statistical rigor and methodological transparency. This study proposes a novel framework combining Large Language Models with conformal prediction for climate vulnerability assessment, achieving distribution-free uncertainty quantification without parametric assumptions. The framework employs a dual-model cross-validation strategy using GPT-4 and Claude to mitigate single-model biases, integrated with split conformal prediction to construct statistically valid prediction intervals. Empirical validation across three Chinese provinces (Guangdong, Sichuan, and Yunnan) demonstrates robust performance, with a calibration correlation coefficient of 0.816, a root mean square error of 7.8, and an actual coverage rate of 94.7% at 95% confidence level. Cross-model validation yields strong consistency (r = 0.883, p < 0.001), while external validation against ND-GAIN (r = −0.70) and INFORM (r = 0.77) indices confirms construct validity. Ablation experiments reveal that the complete framework achieves 16.2% improvement over traditional fixed-weight methods, with LLM integration contributing 11.0% and conformal prediction contributing 4.7% to performance gains. The resulting Climate Vulnerability Scores exhibit clear regional differentiation (Guangdong: 47.8, Sichuan: 63.2, Yunnan: 72.6), corresponding well with socioeconomic development gradients. This reproducible framework provides a transparent and statistically reliable tool for regional climate adaptation planning.
