TI  - A Bayesian analysis of perturbation-based uncertainty estimation for large language models in NLP
AB  - This dissertation studies perturbation-based uncertainty estimation for large language models in natural language processing from a Bayesian perspective, focusing on Inv-Entropy, a recent post-hoc framework based on input-and output-space transition matrices.Inv-Entropy uses the composite kernel P y P x to define an inverse, posterior-like uncertainty score over perturbed input states.The main theoretical contribution is the identification and formalisation of the Perturbation Bottleneck, a structural failure mode in perturbation-based inverse uncertainty estimation.We prove that, if the input-space transition matrix P x is close to the uniform transition matrix, then the composite inverse kernel P y P x is also close to uniform for any row-stochastic output transition matrix P y .Consequently, Inv-Entropy collapses toward its uniform value and becomes largely independent of output-space structure.In Bayesian terms, the constructed inverse update no longer distinguishes between perturbed input states: observing the output does not induce a meaningful posterior-like update over the input geometry.The empirical analysis evaluates Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct on MedQA, TriviaQA, and BioASQ, comparing LLM-based paraphrasing and GAAP perturbations under SBERT and DeBERTa input-space transition models.The results show that Inv-Entropy is regime-dependent.Semantic Entropy has higher AUROC than Inv-Entropy across all TriviaQA conditions.On MedQA, Inv-Entropy is moderately discriminative and GAAP preserves the inverse score, but logit confidence remains strongest.On BioASQ, Inv-Entropy is competitive with Semantic Entropy and achieves its strongest result under GAAP+DeBERTa.These findings refine the theoretical result.The Perturbation Bottleneck is not a claim that Inv-Entropy or GAAP fails universally.Rather, it is a diagnostic condition: non-degenerate P x is necessary, but not sufficient.The perturbation-induced geometry must be semantically informative, meaning taskpreserving, non-degenerate, and aligned with correctness-relevant variation in the model outputs.The dissertation therefore reframes perturbations as part of the statistical object being estimated, instead of as merely auxiliary data augmentation.
