TI  - Uncertainty Quantification in Medical Large Language Models Across Clinical Tasks: A Scoping Review (Preprint)
AB  - BACKGROUND Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly applied to clinical tasks; however, uncertainty estimates remain methodologically heterogeneous, and their translation into clinical decision-making remains unclear. OBJECTIVE To examine how uncertainty quantification (UQ) is implemented, evaluated, reported, and operationalized in medical LLM applications. METHODS We conducted a scoping review of studies published between June 1, 2020, and Nov 1, 2025, by searching PubMed, IEEE Xplore, Scopus, and the ACL Anthology. Eligible studies applied LLMs in medical or clinical settings and reported computable uncertainty measures. Data were extracted on clinical tasks, model access modes, UQ mechanisms, uncertainty signals, evaluation approaches, and workflow integration. We qualitatively synthesized methodological characteristics and summarized within-study quantitative comparisons of calibration and discrimination outcomes where available. RESULTS Of 698 records identified, 12 studies were included in the final synthesis. Identified UQ mechanisms included confidence elicitation, probability elicitation, probability extraction, repeated sampling, ensemble-based methods, multi-agent consensus approaches, and conformal prediction. No consistent relationship was observed between clinical tasks, UQ mechanisms, and uncertainty signals. Only one study directly evaluated the incremental effect of adding UQ relative to a model without uncertainty estimation; deep ensembles improved calibration (median Δ Brier score -0.094 across tasks), whereas Monte Carlo dropout showed smaller and less consistent calibration improvements. Improvements in discrimination were generally modest relative to calibration gains. Integration into decision-support workflows was uncommon; only four studies operationalized uncertainty estimates within predefined decision-support workflows. CONCLUSIONS UQ for medical LLMs remains an emerging field characterized by methodological diversity and substantial heterogeneity in evaluation and reporting practices. The observed variation in relationships between clinical tasks, UQ mechanisms, and uncertainty signals suggests that uncertainty estimates should be selected and interpreted according to the target decision and clinical context. Future work should prioritize transparent frameworks for evaluating, reporting, and linking uncertainty estimates to downstream clinical decisions.
