TI  - OSE: A Constraint-Governed Reasoning Layer for Reducing Hallucination and Overconfident Errors in Large Language Models
AB  - Large language models (LLMs) exhibit strong performance across a wide range of language and reasoning tasks, yet they remain prone to hallucination, false precision, and overconfident errors, particularly in settings involving uncertainty, incomplete evidence, or multi-step inference. This paper introduces OSE (Ontological Structural Engine), a constraint-governed reasoning layer designed to improve the reliability of LLM outputs without modifying the underlying foundation model. OSE operates as a post-model governance mechanism that regulates reasoning progression through atomic constraints, structured verification loops, failure-mode-aware control, and response gating. The paper defines the principal failure modes targeted by OSE, presents its conceptual architecture, and outlines an initial pilot evaluation protocol centered on boundedness, calibration, and practical usefulness. The core claim is intentionally narrow: a properly designed reasoning-governance layer can reduce hallucination, false precision, and confidence-support mismatch while preserving substantial practical value. This work positions post-model reasoning governance as a distinct design direction for building more trustworthy LLM systems under uncertainty.
