TI  - ScaleCal: Scale-Aware Confidence Calibration for Small Language Models
AB  - Abstract Small language models (0.5B--7B) on edge devices must know when to abstain. We present the first systematic cross-scale study of confidence-signal degradation: across eight models from three families and four benchmarks, cheap logit-based signals --- token probability and average log-likelihood -- saturate with growing overconfidence, while normalized entropy collapses to near-random discrimination at 1B and below; sampling consistency remains robust, its cost falling with scale. We formalize this in a signal-to-noise-ratio (SNR) theory -- an SNR--AUROC bridge (AUROC: area under the receiver operating characteristic curve) and a finite-sample conformal guarantee -- and build ScaleCal, a training-free framework that temperature-calibrates the cheap signal and escalates only uncertain queries to $k$-sample semantic consistency, with a conformal threshold fitted from 3-5 scalars. On models up to 3B, ScaleCal reduces expected calibration error (ECE) by 30--50% over the strongest baseline and raises failure-detection AUROC from near-random to 0.84--0.88, at a quarter of a 7B teacher's FLOPs.
