TI  - Dreaming Noise Phase IX: Stability and Semantic Entropy Analysis of Reexive LLM Generation under Stochastic Noise Perturbations
AB  - This study presents Dreaming Noise Phase IX, a novel experimental framework for analyzingsemantic stability, generative drift, and reexive text-generation behavior in modern largelanguage models (LLMs) under controlled stochastic noise perturbations. Across 152 iterative cycles, noise vectors were injected into the prompt structure to observetheir effects on topic consistency, semantic entropy, lexical variation, and rhythmic density.The results demonstrate an 18% reduction in semantic entropy and a 22% improvement in topicstability compared to baseline generations without external noise. These findings suggest thatexogenous noise — when properly tuned and reintroduced iteratively — may counteract internal driftmechanisms within LLMs, promoting long-horizon semantic stability. The work also provides a reproducible methodology including noise seeds, cycle logs, and evaluationmetrics, enabling further research on noise-induced stabilization, reflexive loops, andmeta-generative behaviors in AI systems. This preprint serves as the final version of the Phase IXexperiment within the Dreaming Noise research series.
