TI  - Intrinsic Uncertainty Quantification in Coherence-Gated Hebbian Systems
AB  - Transformer language models process every query through identical frozen weights regardless of whether the input is coherent with the training distribution. Logit confidence is uninformative as an uncertainty signal because it is a function of the output distribution produced by those frozen weights and has no access to whether the current input is structurally coherent with the weight matrices. This is not a limitation of scale; it is a structural consequence of the architecture. Coherence-Gated Hebbian Learning (CGHL) produces three internal signals as mathematical consequences of its dynamics. Coherence $\Phi_t$, the alignment order parameter, decays conditionally in expectation for all sufficiently large $t$ almost surely \citep{abbott2026a}, making it a live monitor of how hard the system works to consolidate a given input. The spectral gap $g_t = \lambda_1(W_t) - \lambda_2(W_t)$ diverges almost surely under adiabatic annealing \citep{abbott2026d}, so that inputs reinforcing the dominant direction increase $g_t$ while out-of-distribution inputs inject mass into orthogonal directions and decrease it. The rank-one error $\|W_t^\perp\|$ decays geometrically for in-distribution inputs and grows for out-of-distribution inputs, bounded by the contraction identity proven in \citep{abbott2026a}. Three internal CGHL signals---coherence recovery $\Phi$, spectral gap perturbation $\Delta g$, and rank-one error---achieve AUROC 1.000 on out-of-distribution detection across all tested conditions, while a parameter-matched transformer baseline achieves AUROC 0.570 using logit confidence. The CGHL memory footprint is $O(N^2)$, constant in context length, eliminating the key-value cache as a structural consequence of the same architectural choice that produces intrinsic uncertainty quantification. A system that knows what it does not know from first principles requires no additional mechanism. It requires the right architecture.
