TI  - No-Reference Point Cloud Quality Assessment via Semantic-Prior Guided Multi-View Sequence Modeling and Uncertainty Quantification
AB  - As a key 3D data format, point clouds (PCs) are widely used in areas such as digital twins and immersive media. However, their visual quality as perceived by humans can be severely degraded by distortions from acquisition, compression, or processing, making point cloud quality assessment (PCQA) crucial. Although existing no-reference methods leverage 3D attributes or multi-view projections, most still evaluate local structures or isolated views independently. They rarely model how cues accumulate across viewpoints into a holistic perception. Beyond visual features, human observers also compare them against semantic expectations, a contrast rarely modelled explicitly in existing methods. Moreover, these methods usually produce deterministic scores, failing to capture the inherent ambiguity in subjective judgments. To this end, we propose PriSeq-UPCQA, a no-reference framework conceptualized in three stages: perception, analysis, and rating. In the perception stage, PCs are projected into multiple views to capture multi-view observations, while six orthogonal views are used to derive semantic priors. In the analysis stage, a pre-trained vision-language model (VLM) activates semantic priors, and the proposed semantic retrieval deviation (SRD) module models the deviation between semantic expectations and multi-view observations. Based on the estimated deviations, the deviation-guided graph traversal (DGGT) strategy organizes candidate viewpoints into a quality-aware observation sequence for subsequent sequence modeling. Finally, the rating stage introduces an uncertainty-aware regression mechanism that predicts both the quality score and aleatoric uncertainty, reflecting prediction reliability and subjective ambiguity. Experiments demonstrate state-of-the-art performance on multiple public databases and confirm the effectiveness of semantic-prior guided deviation modeling, sequence modeling, and uncertainty estimation. The code will be available at https://github.com/LoongSL/PriSeqU-PCQA.
