TI  - Advancing Agentic Self-Assessment: A Critical Review and Extension of Overconfidence in AI Agents
AB  - This review examines the seminal work by Kaddour et al. (2026) on agentic uncertainty, which reveals pervasive overconfidence in AI agents during coding tasks. We summarize the paper's key contributions, including the elicitation of success probabilities at pre-, mid-, and post-execution stages, and the mitigating role of adversarial prompting. Comparisons with related literature highlight alignments with LLM uncertainty estimation (e.g., Kadavath et al., 2022) and divergences from self-correction studies (e.g., Huang et al., 2024). A critical analysis underscores methodological strengths and limitations, such as domain specificity. We extend the framework to non-coding domains like healthcare decision-making and multi-agent systems, proposing hybrid human-AI uncertainty propagation models. This coherent synthesis advances scalable oversight for autonomous AI, emphasizing calibration's role in safety. Mamun, SM (2026)………………………………………1 Advancing Agentic Self-Assessment: A Critical Review and Extension of Overconfidence in AI Agents Also available on Academia.edu: https://www.academia.edu/attachments/131676481/download_file?s=portfolio
